\details{
The full LBA is a race between one accumulator per response option, with the
winner determining both the choice and the RT. With no choice to model there
is nothing to race, so this is the single-accumulator version and the RT is
just that one accumulator's finishing time. All the RT variability comes from
across-trial variability in the start point and the drift rate, rather than
from moment-to-moment noise within the trial.

The threshold is written as an \emph{offset}, \code{b = sigmabias + boundary}, which is
the \code{B} parameterization of \code{DMC} and \code{EMC2} (\code{b = B + A}) rather than the
absolute threshold \code{rtdists} estimates. The threshold has to sit above the
highest possible starting point, and the offset makes \code{b > sigmabias} hold
automatically for any positive value instead of needing an order constraint
between two estimated parameters. See \code{\link[=cogmod_lba2]{cogmod_lba2()}} for the full note. The
cost is that \code{boundary} alone is not the quantity to read off a fitted model;
\code{boundary + sigmabias} is.

\code{sigma} is conventionally \strong{fixed to 1} rather than estimated, because the
evidence scale is arbitrary: multiplying \code{mu}, \code{sigma}, \code{sigmabias} and \code{boundary}
by a common constant leaves the decision time \code{(b - start) / drift} unchanged,
so only ratios are identified and one parameter must be pinned at a non-zero
value to fix the scale. Fix it in the formula with \code{sigma = 1}.
}
\section{The recinormal (LATER) special case}{

Setting \code{sigmabias = 0} removes the start-point variability altogether: the
accumulator starts at zero on every trial, the decision time is \code{b / drift},
and \code{1 / (RT - ndt)} is therefore normally distributed. That is the
\emph{recinormal}, better known in the oculomotor literature as the \strong{LATER}
model of Carpenter and Williams (1995), whose \code{mu} and \code{sigma} are the mean
and SD of \emph{promptness} - the quantity a reciprobit plot puts on its axis.

This is not an approximation reached in the limit. At \code{sigmabias = 0} the
density evaluates to \code{dnorm(b / t, drift, sigma) * b / t^2 / pnorm(drift / sigma)} exactly, to machine precision, in both the R and the Stan
implementation. Two pins are needed rather than one, because zero is the one
value the arbitrary evidence scale leaves alone and so \code{sigmabias} drops off
the scale ray rather than pinning it:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# free: mu and sigma, the mean and SD of promptness
bf(rt ~ 1, sigmabias = 0, boundary = 1)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Because \code{sigmabias} is then a constant rather than a parameter, none of the
trouble described next applies to it, and \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} emits no row for
it.
}

\section{Estimating the start-point range}{

Left free, \code{sigmabias} is estimable but treacherous, precisely because the
recinormal limit above is reached \emph{smoothly}: once the start-point range is
small enough, making it smaller stops changing the density, so the likelihood
goes \strong{flat}. On a \code{softplus} link zero is at minus infinity, so a flat prior
there leaves the posterior improper, and the symptom is a chain that wanders
off rather than one that fails. Fitted without priors on the 4285-trial data
in \code{vignette("rt_models")}, \code{sigmabias} for one condition ran to
\code{softplus(-10.4) = 3e-05} with \code{Rhat} 1.69 and an effective sample size of 6.

There are two ways out, and the choice is a modelling decision rather than a
technical one. Pin \code{sigmabias = 0} and fit the recinormal, which is the honest
option when the design cannot identify a start-point range. Or keep it free
and fence the flat direction off with a prior: \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} does this for
both \code{sigmabias} and \code{boundary} - the threshold is \code{b = sigmabias + boundary},
so the two share the ridge - in the same way and for the same reason it fences
off \code{ndt} and \code{poutlier}. Pass \code{prior = cogmod_priors(f, df)}; the defaults are
weak (\code{normal(0, 1)} on the softplus scale, so a start-point range of roughly
0.3 to 1.3) and are meant to be replaced rather than relied on if you know
more. The two are nested, so \code{loo_compare()} on the two fits is a like-for-like
comparison through the same likelihood.

\code{ndt} and \code{poutlier} mean exactly what they do in \code{\link[=cogmod_lognormal]{cogmod_lognormal()}},
and \code{\link[=with_outliers]{with_outliers()}}, \code{\link[=without_outliers]{without_outliers()}} and \code{\link[=cogmod_priors]{cogmod_priors()}} work here
too. See \code{?rcogmod_lognormal} for the full account.

Note that \code{posterior_epred()} is not available: the decision time has no
finite mean, because \code{E[1 / drift]} diverges for a normal truncated at zero.
}

